": -10.6, "prior_sd": 5.11, "poll_margin": null, "poll_sd": null, "poll_weight": 0.0, "fundamentals": {"margin": -3.55, "lean": -10.0, "environment": 8.3, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -1.85}, "rating_margin": -15.3, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.15, "baseline_margin": -10.6, "analyst_adjustment": -0.4, "key_factors": ["Open R+10 seat with all ten expert ratings at Solid or Safe R", "McKinney holds a dominant money edge: about $2.1 million in outside support versus none for Duresky", "No recent independent polling; the lone February R-sponsored survey showed a 13-point Republican lead", "Polymarket prices the Democratic win odds near 7 percent"], "rationale": "The open seat in a district leaning ten points Republican draws unanimous Solid or Safe R ratings, and McKinney's fundraising and outside-spending advantage dwarfs Duresky's. Weak Democratic resources and market odds near seven percent offset any national-environment tailwind, keeping the race firmly in the Republican column but short of safe.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or an unusually strong Democratic national wave could narrow a margin that currently rests on ratings and fundamentals rather than recent surveys.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Dan Newhouse (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Washington's 4th District is an open seat after Republican Dan Newhouse chose not to run again. The district leans about ten points Republican, and the top-two primary advanced Trump-endorsed Republican Amanda McKinney against Democrat John Duresky. Every major race rating published this fall places the seat in the Republican column, from Likely R to Safe R.\n\nMcKinney has a decisive financial edge, raising roughly $980,000 and benefiting from about $2.1 million in outside support, while Duresky has raised about $180,000 with no outside backing. Prediction markets give Democrats roughly a seven p